  "order": 53,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "00d64n4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "CITIZENSHIP"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Articles 5 to 11 in Part II of the Constitution contain provisions regarding citizenship at the commencement of the Constitution.",
        "Article 11 empowers Parliament to enact legislation regarding citizenship, leading to the enactment of the Citizenship Act, 1955.",
        "Indian citizenship can be acquired in five ways: Birth, Descent, Registration, Naturalisation, and Incorporation of Territory.",
        "Indian citizenship can be lost in three ways: Renunciation, Termination, and Deprivation.",
        "India provides for a single citizenship; acquiring citizenship of a foreign state leads to automatic loss of Indian citizenship under Article 9."
      ],
      "id": "om4ac4n"
    },
